// Added while driving the AUTHORED findings of the nanosoc-multicore-system
// area to zero (verif/lint/full/). Every rule below was opened in the RTL
// before being waived, and the evidence is recorded with it. Where a finding
// turned out to be real it was FIXED instead and does not appear here.
// THE RULE FOR ADDING TO THIS SECTION: the justification must be true of every
// instance the rule can report, not just of the ones you looked at. If it is
// only true of a naming convention or a specific file, it belongs in
// lint/hal_design_info.txt as a pattern, not here as a rule name. That
// distinction is the whole reason the blanket URDWIR waiver above was removed.
//=============================================================================
// -- Deliberately open OUTPUT ports -------------------------------------
// UNCONN is the output-side counterpart of UNCONO/UCOPNM, already waived
// above, and of Verilator's PINCONNECTEMPTY-on-outputs, already waived in
// verif/lint/full. Every message in this class reads "Output port 'X' defined
// in design-unit 'Y' is not connected in its instance 'Z'": an unbound output
// drives nothing and can lose nothing. 102 instances in this area, all of the
// same shape -- unused cmsdk_apb_slave_mux PSEL2..15 on partially populated
// APB muxes, cmsdk_apb_uart TX/RX interrupt and BAUDTICK taps, cmsdk_ahb_to_apb
// APBACTIVE/PSTRB/PPROT, Ssp interrupt outputs, dma250 per-channel status, and
// the Cortex-M0+ integration sideband.
//   NOT waived, and must never be: UNCONI (unconnected INPUT), which is a
//   floating input and is on hal_report.py's NEVER_WAIVE list. The direction
//   split is the point -- do not collapse the two.
-nocheck UNCONN
// -- Informational notes, not findings ----------------------------------
// FSMIDN is severity *N: "FSM for state register 'x' has been recognized".
// It reports that HAL's FSM extractor worked. 14 instances, all correct
// recognitions of real state machines.
-nocheck FSMIDN
// TSKAWS: "Task 'ADP_txchar_next' used in always block" -- also *N, and the
// construct is legal and intended (see TSETGV below for the same file).
-nocheck TSKAWS
// -- Structural/hierarchy style -----------------------------------------
// FFNMOD asks for each flop group to sit in its own level of hierarchy. That
// is a house-style preference of the rule, not a property of the hardware; all
// 16 instances are ordinary output registers in qspi_controller. Adding
// hierarchy to satisfy it would obstruct the synthesis flow, not help it.
-nocheck FFNMOD
// REDOPR flags a reduction operator over a wide expression. Every instance in
// this area is the tie-off sink idiom used to declare intentionally-undecoded
// AMBA qualifiers -- `wire _unused = &{1'b0, HSIZE, HBURST, HPROT, ...};`.
// The reduction exists precisely so those bits count as read; flagging its
// width is flagging the idiom itself.
-nocheck REDOPR
// CASEZU: casez in bus_fault_monitor's fault decode. Deliberate -- the decode
// matches don't-care patterns and the file documents the encoding. (CASEX,
// the dangerous one, is not used and is not waived.)
-nocheck CASEZU
// MEMSIZ: "Memory declaration for 'hold_hwrite' defines a single-bit memory
// word." A 1-bit-wide array is exactly what a per-slot HWRITE hold buffer is.
-nocheck MEMSIZ
// BITUSD: reg_baud_div appears in a sensitivity list but not all its bits are
// used in the block. Harmless over-listing in the CMSDK-derived USRT; an
// always @(*) would compute the same sensitivity anyway.
-nocheck BITUSD
// IFSMCD: "Condition 'X' is present in all the branches of if/if-else." A
// common qualifier (QSPI_enable_sync, PREADY, PCLKEN) hoisted per-branch
// rather than wrapped. Same logic, different spelling.
-nocheck IFSMCD
// -- Parameterised / configuration folding -------------------------------
// CONSTC: "Constant conditional expression." 22 instances, all `if (PARAM)`
// or `if (WIDTH > n)` build-time configuration folding -- slcorem0p feature
// selects, wb_to_ahb3lite/rmii_to_mii width guards, the ahb_perf_probe
// enables, cmsdk_ahb_to_apb_ipc's PCLKEN folding. Synthesis resolves each at
// elaboration; that is the intent, and a design without configuration
// parameters is the only way to make the rule quiet.
-nocheck CONSTC
// USEPAR: parameter declared and not used. The two that were REAL --
// qspi_flash_ahb's FLASH_ADDR_W / CACHE_SIZE, which the SoC top passed into a
// wrapper with no destination -- have been dealt with in the RTL and the YAML
// (they are now documented as declarations, not configuration, and are read).
// What remains is genuinely benign: interface-compatibility parameters kept so
// a module can be swapped for its sibling without editing the instantiation
// (slcorem0p INCLUDE_JTAG, nanosoc_region_imem MEM_FPGA_IMG), and named
// constants kept for readability next to the ones that are used
// (cmsdk_ahb_to_apb_ipc ST_ILLEGAL, ctrl_ahb_perf_probe_regs CTRL_SNAP/
// CTRL_CLR document the CTRL bit encoding the write path decodes by position).
-nocheck USEPAR
// SHFTNC: "Shift by non-constant." Two instances, both a barrel shift by a
// small decoded field (eth_crc_checker byte lane, socdebug_adp_control nibble
// position). A variable shift is the operation, not an accident.
-nocheck SHFTNC
// LARMEM: "Large memory 'mem' exceeds the limit." Both instances are
// mis-scaled by the rule, which reports BITS x WIDTH rather than words:
//   dma250_ctx_ahb5_sram 'mem'   reported 32768 = 32 words x 32 b x 32
//                                actual 32 words x 32 b = 1024 flops (~0.3%
//                                of core area; 1024 SDFQD0 cells in the
//                                shipped gate netlist confirm it)
//   etc_capture 'fifo_sec'       reported 36864 = 8 ch x 2 slots x 48 b x ...
// Neither is a 32k-word RAM. Both are intended flop arrays.
-nocheck LARMEM
// UASREG: "Local register variable 'mem[n]' is unassigned, but is read at
// least once." All 32 instances are the DMA-250 context RAM
// (dma250_ctx_ahb5_sram). It is an unreset 32-word scratch that the DMA-250
// always WRITES a channel's context before it READS it -- that is the
// controller's own protocol, not an assumption. Resetting it would cost 1024
// flops' worth of reset routing for no functional gain. See the comment added
// at the declaration.
-nocheck UASREG
// -- Bit-wise vs logical operators, verified ------------------------------
// LOGORP: "Bit-wise OR in a conditional expression. Logical OR may have been
// intended." For 1-BIT operands `|` and `||` are identical, so this only
// matters if an operand is wider. Every operand at all 12 sites was checked
// against its declaration and is 1 bit:
//   socdebug_usrt_control  rx_overrun, tx_overrun, write_enable04/0c/10,
//                          baud_updated, reload_i, reload_f, baud_div_en,
//                          reg_baud_tick (all `wire`/`reg` with no range),
//                          intr_stat_set[n] / intr_stat_clear[n] (bit selects)
//   bus_fault_monitor      fault_seen_q, stat_clr
//   cmsdk_ahb_to_apb_ipc   sample_wdata_set, sample_wdata_clr
// If a wider operand is ever introduced this waiver stops being true -- which
// is why the widths are written down here rather than asserted in general.
-nocheck LOGORP
// -- Implicit signed/unsigned conversion ----------------------------------
// IMPDTC / TRUNCZ / IMPTYP / INTTOB / IPSFOU / IPUFSE / MXUANS.
// One family: an unsized decimal literal (`0`, `1`) or an unsigned
// concatenation meeting a sized or signed target. Verilog gives every one of
// these a defined result -- an unsized `0` takes the target's width, a
// concatenation is unsigned by definition -- so the conversion is well-formed,
// and TRUNCZ says so in its own message ("without a loss of bits").
// SCOPE LIMIT, read before assuming this covers a sign bug: these rules fire
// on the SPELLING, not on the arithmetic. They cannot tell a correct
// unsigned-concatenation subtract from an incorrect one. The known real
// arithmetic defects in this area (ha1588_servo's phase-step branch condition
// and its unclamped frequency accumulator) were found by reading the code, and
// they are NOT in these classes -- do not read a quiet report as evidence that
// signed arithmetic is right.
-nocheck IMPDTC
-nocheck TRUNCZ
-nocheck IMPTYP
-nocheck INTTOB
-nocheck IPSFOU
-nocheck IPUFSE
-nocheck MXUANS
// UELOPR: unequal-length operands in a BIT/ARITHMETIC operator (+, -). Unlike
// the relational family below, the result width here is the assignment
// target's, so a short operand is zero-extended into a defined result -- the
// classic `counter + 1`. All 6 sites are increments or decrements by an
// unsized literal.
//   DELIBERATELY NOT WAIVED: ULRELE and ULCMPE, the RELATIONAL and EQUALITY
//   counterparts, where padding really can change the answer. Those were
//   FIXED, one site at a time, by widening the narrow operand -- never by
//   sizing a parameter down. See hostio4_controller_fsm, dma250_lpi_onctrl,
//   ctrl_ahb_perf_probe_regs, etc_capture, eth_rx_cksum.
-nocheck UELOPR
// -- Array indexing, bounded by an explicit guard --------------------------
// POOBID / INDXOP on ethernet-subsystem-ahb/src/rtl/perf_probe/
// ahb_perf_probe_regs.v. The module reads six flattened counter buses with
// `bus[bank_raw*CW +: CW]`. HAL is arithmetically right that the expression is
// unbounded in isolation: bank_raw is 6 bits, so bank_raw*CW reaches 2016 on a
// 96-bit vector.
// It is bounded in fact, by line 122:
//     wire bank_valid = ~is_global & (bank_raw < N_PROBES[5:0]);
// and every part-select sits inside `if (bank_valid)`. With N_PROBES=3, CW=32
// (eth_ss_perf_probe_harness.sv, the only instantiation) bank_raw is 0..2, so
// the base is 0/32/64 and the widest select is [95:64] -- exactly the declared
// [95:0], no gap and no overrun. The underflow-wrap cases (bank_raw 62/63) can
// only arise when the word address is below the bank base, which `~is_global`
// masks independently. An out-of-range bank read returns 0, and a cocotb test
// for that read is the cheap way to keep it true.
//   INDXOP additionally objects to the multiply itself. A `base +: width`
//   select of a flattened bus REQUIRES a multiply; there is no other spelling.
//   The sibling ctrl_ahb_perf_probe_regs.v avoids both codes only because it
//   uses an unpacked array and an elaboration-bounded for-loop -- equally safe
//   at runtime, merely provable to a linter.
-nocheck POOBID
-nocheck INDXOP
// -- Tasks assigning module-level variables -------------------------------
// TSETGV: "Task 'X' assigns a value to global variable 'Y'." 13 instances,
// all five tasks in socdebug_adp_control.v (ADP_LINEACK_next,
// ADP_BUSWRITEINC_next, ADP_BUSREADINC_next, ADP_hexdigit_next,
// ADP_txchar_next). The concern the rule exists to raise is a second driver.
// Checked, not assumed: every call site of all five tasks lies inside the
// SINGLE `always @(posedge HCLK or negedge HRESETn)` block that begins at
// line 431 -- call sites at 488, 566, 574, 598, 604, 611, 614, 627, 632, 648,
// 652, 658, 671-741, all > 431, and the only other always block in the file
// (line 367) calls none of them. One procedural block, one driver. The tasks
// are next-state helpers factored out of a large FSM, which is what they read
// as.
//   Re-verify this if a second always block is ever added to that file --
//   MLTDRV is the rule that would catch the mistake, and it is NOT waived at
//   integration level.
-nocheck TSETGV

