// ===== from tidechart/lint/hal.tcl  (1 rule(s))
// TideChart HAL Lint Waivers
// Cadence HAL (Xcelium) rule configuration for the TideChart unit lint flow
// (`tidechart/lint/Makefile`) and, by union, for the full-chiplet pass in
// `verif/lint/full/` (see that README: `hal_rules.tcl` is the merge of every
// unit-level `lint/hal.tcl` in the integration).
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SIGN-OFF INVARIANT
// Nothing in this file may waive a sign-off rule. The classes that must stay
// live in authored RTL are, at minimum:
//   MLTDRV  CLKDMN  INSYNC  GLTASR  UNCONI  SIZMIS
//   ULRELE  ULCMPE                       (padding produces an incorrect result)
//   any *E in authored RTL
//   any empty-or-omitted pin on an input or inout
// ULRELE and ULCMPE are the reason: they are the only width classes that assert
// a WRONG RESULT rather than a style deviation, and TideLink's own README ties
// the class to a real silicon finding. Every ULRELE/ULCMPE site in TideChart was
// FIXED (2026-08-09), not waived -- always by widening the narrow operand, never
// by narrowing the wide one. If one reappears, it is a new defect.
// Every waiver below records WHY. A waiver without a justification is a bug you
// have agreed to keep. If the justification stops being true, delete the line.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Contributors
//   David Mapstone (d.a.mapstone@soton.ac.uk)
// Copyright 2026, SoC Labs (www.soclabs.org)
// -- Pre-existing STARC waivers, PARKED --------------------------------------
// This file is consumed by `hal -file`, which is HAL's command-file syntax --
// `//` comments and `-nocheck <RULE>` directives -- and that is the form every
// other unit ruleset in the integration uses (tidelink, ethernet-mac-ahb,
// nanosoc-multicore-system, ...). The two lines below were written in Tcl
// `hal::rule_waive` form, which is why `verif/lint/full/hal_rules.tcl` records
// TideChart as "(rule_waive form, see below)" and folded neither of them into
// the merged ruleset: they have never reached the integration pass.
// They are parked rather than deleted -- they name STARC ruleset entries, not
// HAL rule codes, and the unit Makefile runs `-check ALL_RTL`, not STARC, so
// they are no-ops there too. Restore them (in whichever form the STARC flow
// wants) if STARC checking is ever turned on; do not leave them live in a
// command file that HAL parses positionally.
//   hal::rule_waive -rulename STARC05-2.1.3.1 \
//       -comment "Parameterized port arrays for multi-port design"
//   hal::rule_waive -rulename STARC-2.6.1.1 \
//       -comment "LFSR feedback taps in election FSM"
// ==========================================================================
// Width / arithmetic classes
// ==========================================================================
// UELOPR -- unequal-length operand in a bit/arithmetic operator.
// Every remaining instance in TideChart is the same shape: a register that is
// ID_W (5) or PORT_W (3) bits wide, minus an UNSIZED literal 1, feeding an
// array index -- e.g. `root_hop_count_arr[hop_target_r - 1]`,
// `route_table[read_addr - 1]`. The literal makes the subtraction 32-bit, so
// index 0 underflows to 32'hFFFFFFFF, which every reader guards with an
// explicit `(addr == 0) ? <const> : table[addr-1]` ternary.
// "Fixing" this by doing the arithmetic at the index width is the WRONG
// direction and would manufacture a bug: 5'd0 - 5'd1 is 5'd31, an IN-RANGE
// entry of a 31-entry table, so the underflow would stop being harmless and
// start aliasing onto a real route. The alternative -- zero-extending each
// register to 32 bits -- is 20+ edits across a peer-facing FSM that changes no
// behaviour and no netlist. Neither is worth it; the class is not the
// wrong-result class (that is ULRELE, which is fixed above).
// (already waived by nanosoc-multicore-system/lint/hal.tcl): UELOPR
// POOBID -- variable index/range selection potentially outside the range.
// HAL cannot bound a dynamic index through a guard, so it reports every
// guarded one. The surviving instances are:
//   tidechart_route_table.sv:108-124   `(addr == 0) ? const : table[addr-1]`
//                                      addr is ID_W bits, table has 31 entries,
//                                      so 1..31 -> 0..30, all in range.
//   tidechart_enum_fsm.sv (root_parent_id / root_hop_count_arr / root_egress /
//                          root_uplink_arr / link_active)
//                                      same `id-1` shape, same guards.
// The unguarded ones were real and are FIXED, not waived:
//   crossbar enum_fc_tx_port  -> bounds-checked (A5)
//   enum_fsm assign_port      -> bounds-checked
//   every scan pointer        -> indexed at $clog2(NUM_PORTS) width
// A POOBID on a NEWLY unguarded index is therefore still a finding worth
// having; this waiver keeps the class from burying it.
// (already waived by nanosoc-multicore-system/lint/hal.tcl): POOBID
// MXUANS -- expression mixes signed and unsigned operands.
// The only instances are `for (pi = 0; pi < NUM_CHIPLETS_MAX; ...)` integer
// loop variables compared against unsigned registers. The loop bounds make pi
// provably non-negative. Fixed at the one site HAL also flagged ULRELE on;
// the residue is the loop-variable idiom itself.
// (already waived by nanosoc-multicore-system/lint/hal.tcl): MXUANS
// ==========================================================================
// Structural / declaration classes
// ==========================================================================
// USEPAR -- parameter declared and unused.
// TideChart's sub-modules are instantiated with ONE uniform parameter list
// from tidechart_controller.sv so that a configuration change is a single
// edit at the top. Removing FC_DATA_W / PUF_ENABLE / ACCEL_COUNT /
// SRAM_BLOCK_COUNT / PUF_NUM_WORDS / RST_WAIT_RT_ACK / PAYLOAD_W from the
// modules that do not currently read them would break those instantiations and
// re-break them the next time a phase starts using one. Note this is NOT the
// same as the "dead configuration knob" class (plan B6, qspi_flash_ahb): these
// parameters are consumed by siblings in the same instantiation.
// (already waived by nanosoc-multicore-system/lint/hal.tcl): USEPAR
// DFDRVS -- sub-parts of a vector driven by different assignment types.
// Register banks where the reset branch writes the whole vector and the APB
// write branch writes named bit fields (timeout_reg_r, cong_bcast_period_r,
// cong_jitter_mask_r, lfsr_r, free_counter_r, own_class_r). Single procedural
// driver in every case -- confirmed by MLTDRV = 0 across the chiplet -- so this
// is a description-style note, not a multi-driver.
// (already waived by nanosoc-multicore-system/lint/hal.tcl): DFDRVS
// MEMSIZ -- memory declaration with a single-bit word.
// `secure_table` / `valid_table` (tidechart_route_table.sv) and `seen_valid_r`
// (tidechart_link_state_agent.sv) are deliberately one flag per entry, sized by
// the same NUM_ENTRIES as the tables they qualify. HAL's "check for an error in
// the register declaration" is the right question and the answer is no.
// (already waived by nanosoc-multicore-system/lint/hal.tcl): MEMSIZ
// LARMEM -- large memory note.
// HAL's LARMEM figure is bits x width, not words (see
// docs/LINT_REMEDIATION_PLAN.md §0, where the same class was misread as a 1 Mib
// DMA context RAM and was actually 1 024 flops). root_port_subtree_r and
// root_below_r are NUM_CHIPLETS_MAX x NUM_CHIPLETS_MAX bit masks. This is a
// note (*N), not a warning, and the area is accounted for in the netlist.
// (already waived by nanosoc-multicore-system/lint/hal.tcl): LARMEM
// NAUTOF is NOT waived -- all four static functions were given `automatic`
// (2026-08-09), matching tidechart_link_state_agent.sv, which already did.
// ==========================================================================
// Intentional-structure classes
// ==========================================================================
// FDTHRU -- combinational feedthrough from an input to an output.
// tidechart_crossbar is a crossbar: routing an input port's data to a selected
// output with no register in between is its entire function, and the AXI-Stream
// contract requires the ready/valid path to be combinational. The same applies
// to the link-state agent's bcast_rx_data[31:27] -> rt_hop_addr lookup tap.
// (already waived by nanosoc-multicore-system/lint/hal.tcl): FDTHRU
// SHFTNC -- shift by a non-constant.
// The LFSR step, the FC path-field pack (`cur_port_idx_r << (hop*PORT_W)`) and
// the chiplet-bitmap builds (`1 << id`) are all variable shifts by
// construction. Shift amounts are bounded by the field widths they index.
// (already waived by nanosoc-multicore-system/lint/hal.tcl): SHFTNC
// CONSTC -- constant conditional expression.
// `if (PUF_ENABLE)` / `if (NUM_PORTS > 1)` configuration folding. The shipping
// build sets PUF_ENABLE=0 and NUM_PORTS=1, so these fold at elaboration exactly
// as intended -- that is what a generate-time parameter is for.
// (already waived by nanosoc-multicore-system/lint/hal.tcl): CONSTC
// MULOPR is NOT waived -- the three `PUF_ENABLE &&` sites were rewritten as
// `(PUF_ENABLE != 0) &&` (2026-08-09), the form the same file already used.
// FUSEGV -- function reads a module-scope variable.
// tidechart_link_state_agent::build_packet reads link_active and
// local_link_state_i deliberately: it builds a broadcast packet FROM the
// module's current link state, and passing 2 x NUM_PORTS values in as arguments
// would obscure that without changing the logic. The function is `automatic`
// and combinational.
-nocheck FUSEGV
// IFSMCD -- the same condition appears in all branches of an if/else-if chain.
// One instance: the subtree write-port decode in tidechart_apb_regs.sv, where
// the chain is `port < NUM_PORTS` / `port == NUM_PORTS` / else, i.e. a
// three-way range decode written as a chain. HAL is pattern-matching the shared
// left-hand operand, not finding redundant logic.
// (already waived by nanosoc-multicore-system/lint/hal.tcl): IFSMCD
// ==========================================================================
// Reviewed and deliberately NOT waived by rule -- recorded here so the next
// reader does not have to re-derive it
// ==========================================================================
// TERMST (*E) at tidechart_election_fsm.sv -- "FSM for state register 'state_r'
// has terminal states 4".
// This finding is FACTUALLY WRONG about this design and is left visible rather
// than waived, because it is an *E and the sign-off rule is that no *E in
// authored RTL gets silenced.
// ST_SETTLED (3'd4) does have an exit arc. It is in the SEQUENTIAL block:
//     always @(posedge clk or negedge resetn)
//         if (!resetn)      state_r <= ST_IDLE;
//         else if (restart) state_r <= ST_IDLE;   // I8 soft re-arm, TC_CTRL[3]
//         else              state_r <= state_next;
// HAL extracted the FSM from the combinational next-state function alone, where
// ST_SETTLED does self-loop, and never saw the restart override. The exit is
// reachable from firmware (TC_CTRL.RESET) and is exercised by the election
// cocotb environment.
// Moving `restart` into the next-state function would make HAL see the arc, and
// was REJECTED as a fix: `state_next` is also consumed at
// tidechart_election_fsm.sv:133 as the LFSR reseed trigger
// (`state_r == ST_WAIT_LINKS && state_next == ST_CLAIM_TX`). Folding restart
// into state_next would suppress that reseed on a restart that coincides with
// ST_WAIT_LINKS -- a real behaviour change, to satisfy a false positive.

